tel 1.0

base surveillance
import soundness

info   watching directories and files for changes, as a scoped stream of typed events
detail
    _Surveillance_ watches the filesystem. A path, or a collection of paths, is *opened* as
    `Watch`, and within the block a `Watch.Handle` yields the changes as they happen — a file
    created, modified or deleted, a directory created — as a lazy `Chain` of `Watch.Event`s,
    or debounced into `Watch.Batch`es once the tree has been quiet for a while. The
    operating-system registration lasts exactly as long as the block, and capture checking
    confines the handle and its streams to it.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
    import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import temporaryDirectories.systemTemporaryDirectory

    val directory: Path on Local = temporaryDirectory[Path on Local] / "watched"

    directory.open[Watch](): watcher ?=>
      watcher.stream.each:
        case NewFile(dir, file) => Out.println(t"created $file")
        case Modify(dir, file)  => Out.println(t"modified $file")
        case Delete(dir, file)  => Out.println(t"deleted $file")
        case _                  => ()
    ```

    Change detection is delegated to a `Watcher` backend. With no import, `Watcher.default`
    applies: `JavaBaseWatcher`, over the operating system's own notification service
    (`java.nio.file.WatchService`). Where that is unavailable or unreliable — some network or
    container filesystems — a `PollingWatcher`, made by `watchers.polling(interval)`, diffs
    snapshots of each directory instead, and is chosen by binding it as a local given.

    A watch covers a directory's immediate entries, not its whole subtree; a path naming a
    file watches its parent directory, filtered to that one name. Several paths opened
    together share one event stream.

    The library has one source set, `surveillance.core`, for the JVM, built on parasite's
    supervised tasks and turbulence's `Relay`. Everything is exported to the `soundness`
    umbrella, with the `watchers` object mirrored as a package; its `javaBaseWatcher` given is
    imported by name, since `import watchers.*` does not import givens.

refer surveillance.Watch
refer surveillance.Watcher
refer surveillance.watchers
refer aperture.open

entry .Watch   a registration of watched paths, its events, and the `open[Watch]` form
  detail
      `Watch` is the form a path is opened as to watch it, and the type of a live
      registration. The usual way in is `path.open[Watch]() { watcher ?=> … }`, through
      `.openable` (or `.allOpenable` for a collection of paths), which registers the paths,
      lends a `Watch.Handle` to the block, and unregisters when the block ends however it
      ends.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
      import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import temporaryDirectories.systemTemporaryDirectory

      val sources: Path on Local = temporaryDirectory[Path on Local] / "src"
      val tests: Path on Local = temporaryDirectory[Path on Local] / "test"

      List(sources, tests).open[Watch](): watcher ?=>
        watcher.stream.take(1).each(event => Out.println(event.path[Path on Local].encode))
      ```

      A `Watch` value may also be made directly with `Watch(paths)`, outside any scope; then
      `#unregister` must be called to cancel the registration and terminate `#stream`, which
      otherwise never ends. Watching is pure observation, so no handle operation is
      grant-gated: the mode passed to `open` is irrelevant, and the default, `Read`,
      describes it best. There are no flags.

      Registration raises `Watch.Error` when a path does not exist, is not a directory, may
      not be watched by this user, or would exceed the operating system's limit on watches.
  refer surveillance.Watch.Handle
  refer surveillance.Watch.Event
  refer surveillance.Watcher
  entry .apply   registers any collection of paths with the `Watcher` in scope, returning a live `Watch`
      `Watch(paths)` takes any `Traversable` collection of path values which are `Abstractable
      across Paths to Text` — a Soundness `List(…)` or a stdlib `Iterable` — and needs a
      `Watcher` and a `Tactic[Watch.Error]`. Each directory is watched whole; each other path
      watches its parent directory, filtered to its own name; and paths in the same directory
      share one registration. The result captures the watcher, and must be unregistered
      with `Watch#unregister`; `open[Watch]` does that itself.
  entry .openable   opens a single path as `Watch`: `path.open[Watch]() { watcher ?=> … }`
      The `Openable` given for any path type `Abstractable across Paths to Text`, in the
      companion and so in implicit scope. It needs a `Watcher` (the default suffices) and a
      `Tactic[Watch.Error]`, and lends the block a `Watch.Handle`, unregistering when the
      block ends.
  entry .allOpenable   opens a collection of paths as `Watch`, with one event stream for all of them
      The `Openable` given for any `Iterable` of paths: `List(a, b).open[Watch]() { … }`. It is
      parameterized over the concrete collection type, since an `Openable`'s `Self` is matched
      invariantly. Needs a `Watcher` and a `Tactic[Watch.Error]`.
  entry .Openable   the `Openable` instance behind `.openable`, as a named class
      A `path is Openable in Watch` with no flags (`Operand` is `Nothing`) and a `Watch.Handle`
      as its handle. Named rather than anonymous, since an anonymous instance freshens the
      handle's capability type in its inferred `Result`.
  entry .AllOpenable   the `Openable` instance behind `.allOpenable`, for a collection of paths
      As `Watch.Openable`, with a collection of paths as its `Self`.
  entry .Handle   the scoped capability an `open[Watch]` block receives, with the watch's streams
    detail
        The handle lent by `path.open[Watch]()`. It is an exclusive capability confined to
        the block, so neither it nor a stream derived from it can outlive the registration.
        Inside the block it is the contextual parameter, and the top-level `watch` gives it
        by name.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        import soundness.*
        import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
        import stdios.javaLangSystemStdio
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import temporaryDirectories.systemTemporaryDirectory

        val directory: Path on Local = temporaryDirectory[Path on Local] / "watched"

        directory.open[Watch]():
          watch.batches(0.2*Second).each: batch =>
            batch.paths[Path on Local].each(path => Out.println(path.encode))
        ```
    entry #stream   every event, one at a time, as it arrives
        A lazy `Chain` of `Watch.Event`s which blocks for the next event and ends when the
        registration is cancelled. It is a single-owner drain of the event queue: two
        traversals compete for the same events.
    entry #batches   events coalesced into one `Watch.Batch` whenever the tree has been quiet for a while
        `batches(quiet)` takes any duration value (`0.2*Second`) and yields a `Watch.Batch`
        each time no event has arrived for that long, so a burst of saves from an editor is
        reported once, after it has settled.
  entry .Event   one change to a watched directory: a file created, modified or deleted, or a directory created
    detail
        Each event records the watched directory, `#dir`, as text, and the name of the entry
        within it that changed; `#path` combines the two as a path type of the caller's
        choosing. The four cases are also exported at the package level, so `case
        NewFile(dir, file) =>` matches without the `Watch.Event` prefix.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val event: Watch.Event = Watch.Event.NewFile(t"/x", t"a.txt")

        event match
          case NewFile(dir, file)          => t"created $file in $dir"
          case NewDirectory(dir, name)     => t"created directory $name"
          case Modify(dir, file)           => t"modified $file"
          case Delete(dir, file)           => t"deleted $file"
        ```

        The operating-system backend reports a modification however the platform does,
        which may be several events for one save; the polling backend reports one
        modification per changed size or modification time between scans, and cannot see a
        change which leaves both alone. Neither reports a modification of a directory.
    entry .NewFile   a file, or anything not a directory, was created in a watched directory
      entry #dir    the watched directory, as text
      entry #file   the new entry's name within the directory
    entry .NewDirectory   a directory was created in a watched directory
      entry #dir         the watched directory, as text
      entry #directory   the new directory's name within the watched one
    entry .Modify   a file in a watched directory was modified
      entry #dir    the watched directory, as text
      entry #file   the modified file's name within the directory
    entry .Delete   an entry was deleted from a watched directory
      entry #dir    the watched directory, as text
      entry #file   the deleted entry's name within the directory
    entry #dir   the watched directory in which the change happened, as text
    entry #path   the changed entry's full path, as a path type of the caller's choosing
        `event.path[Path on Local]` (or any `Instantiable across Paths from Text`) joins the
        directory and the entry's name and normalizes the result.
  entry .Batch   the events of one quiet period, oldest first, as `Watch.Handle#batches` yields them
    detail
        A burst of activity, ended by a period with no events. `#paths` lists the paths
        touched, once each, which is what a rebuild-on-change loop usually wants.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val batch = Watch.Batch(List(NewFile(t"/x", t"a"), Modify(t"/x", t"a"), NewFile(t"/x", t"b")))
        batch.paths[Path on Local]   // List(/x/a, /x/b)
        ```
    entry #events   the batch's events, oldest first
    entry #paths   every path touched in the batch, once each, in order of first occurrence
  entry .Error   raised when a path cannot be watched
    detail
        Error 545: _the path could not be watched because …_, with one of four reasons:
        `LimitExceeded` (_the operating system's limit on watched paths has been exceeded_,
        such as inotify's `max_user_watches`), `Nonexistent` (_the path does not exist_),
        `NotDirectory` (_the path is not a directory_, as when a watched path's parent is a
        regular file) and `PermissionDenied` (_the user does not have permission to watch the
        path_). Raised by `Watch(paths)` and by `open[Watch]` as the registration is made.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem

        val target = t"/nonexistent/child".as[Path on Local]
        capture[Watch.Error](target.open[Watch]() { () }).reason   // Watch.Error.Reason.Nonexistent
        ```
    entry .Reason   why a path could not be watched: a limit, a missing path, a non-directory or a permission
      detail
          Each case's sub-number of error 545 is its `#number`:

          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `LimitExceeded`  1  _the operating system's limit on watched paths has been exceeded_
          row  `Nonexistent`  2  _the path does not exist_
          row  `NotDirectory`  3  _the path is not a directory_
          row  `PermissionDenied`  4  _the user does not have permission to watch the path_
          ```
      entry #number   the reason's sub-number of error 545
    entry .communicable   renders each reason as the phrase completing _the path could not be watched because …_
    entry #reason   why the path could not be watched
  entry #stream   every event, one at a time, until the watch is unregistered
      A lazy `Chain` of `Watch.Event`s which ends when `#unregister` is called. Traversing it
      blocks for each next event.
  entry #batches   events coalesced into a `Watch.Batch` after each quiet period of the length given
  entry #unregister   cancels the registration and ends the event streams
      Stops the backend delivering events for this watch's directories and terminates
      `#stream` and `#batches`. `open[Watch]` calls it when its block ends.

entry .Watcher   the backend which detects filesystem changes: the operating system's service, or polling
  detail
      A `Watcher` turns a set of directories into a `Watcher.Registration` which puts events
      into a relay as they happen. It is a capability, since a backend runs supervised tasks
      of its own. With no import, `.default` — `JavaBaseWatcher`, the operating system's
      notification service — is used; a different backend is a local given:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import temporaryDirectories.systemTemporaryDirectory

      given Watcher = watchers.polling(0.05*Second)

      val directory: Path on Local = temporaryDirectory[Path on Local] / "watched"

      directory.open[Watch](): watcher ?=>
        watcher.stream.head
      ```

      Since the default given is a capability, code which reaches it captures it; a test body
      that does so runs inline rather than deferred.
  refer surveillance.JavaBaseWatcher
  refer surveillance.PollingWatcher
  refer surveillance.watchers
  entry .default   `JavaBaseWatcher`, the backend used when no other `Watcher` is in scope
      In the companion, so in implicit scope with no import; a local given or an imported
      `watchers.javaBaseWatcher` takes precedence over it.
  entry .Registration   one backend registration, which `#cancel` ends
    detail
        What `Watcher#watch` returns: cancelling it stops delivery of further events for the
        directories registered in that call. `Watch#unregister` cancels it.
    entry #cancel   stops delivering events for the registered directories
  entry #watch   registers directories, each with a filter on entry names, delivering events to a relay
      `watch(directories, spool)`: each directory is paired with a pure predicate on the names
      of its entries, and events for matching names are put into the `Relay`. Raises
      `Watch.Error` if a directory cannot be registered. `Watch(paths)` builds the filters and
      calls it; a user rarely calls it directly.

entry .JavaBaseWatcher   the `Watcher` over the operating system's notification service, the default
    Wraps `java.nio.file.WatchService` (inotify on Linux, the platform's equivalent
    elsewhere). One service and one polling task are shared by every registration in the
    process, and the service is closed once nothing is being watched. A watched directory
    which is deleted drops out of the registry. Registration failures map to `Watch.Error`:
    a missing path to `Nonexistent`, a file to `NotDirectory`, a refused access to
    `PermissionDenied`, and any other I/O failure — in practice, the platform's watch limit —
    to `LimitExceeded`. It is `Watcher.default`, and `watchers.javaBaseWatcher` names it.

entry .PollingWatcher   a `Watcher` which diffs snapshots of each directory at a fixed interval
    `PollingWatcher(interval)`, usually made through `watchers.polling(interval)`, with any
    duration value. Every interval it lists each watched directory, records each entry's kind,
    modification time and size, and compares with the previous scan to report creations,
    modifications and deletions. It needs no operating-system support, at the cost of latency
    bounded by the interval and blindness to a change which leaves size and modification time
    as they were. Each registration has its own background task, interrupted when it is
    cancelled; registration raises `Watch.Error` with `Nonexistent` or `NotDirectory` when a
    path is missing or is not a directory.

entry .watch   the `Watch.Handle` in scope, inside an `open[Watch]` block
    `watch` returns the contextual handle with its precise capturing type, so a block may
    leave its handle parameter unnamed, in the manner of galilei's `file`:

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import filesystemBackends.javaBaseFilesystem
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import temporaryDirectories.systemTemporaryDirectory

    val directory: Path on Local = temporaryDirectory[Path on Local] / "watched"

    directory.open[Watch]():
      watch.stream.head
    ```

entry .NewFile   `Watch.Event.NewFile`, exported for matching: a file was created in a watched directory
    The event case, exported to the package so a match may write `case NewFile(dir, file)`.
    `dir` is the watched directory and `file` the new entry's name within it.
entry .NewDirectory   `Watch.Event.NewDirectory`, exported for matching: a directory was created
    The event case, exported to the package so a match may write `case NewDirectory(dir,
    name)`.
entry .Modify   `Watch.Event.Modify`, exported for matching: a file in a watched directory was modified
    The event case, exported to the package so a match may write `case Modify(dir, file)`.
entry .Delete   `Watch.Event.Delete`, exported for matching: an entry was deleted from a watched directory
    The event case, exported to the package so a match may write `case Delete(dir, file)`.

entry .watchers   the `Watcher` backends, as a given to import by name or a value to bind as one
  detail
      Which backend detects changes is decided by the `Watcher` given in scope; with none,
      `Watcher.default` applies. This object, mirrored as the `watchers` package in the
      umbrella, holds the alternatives: `javaBaseWatcher`, to name the default explicitly,
      and `polling`, a method which makes a polling backend for an interval.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Default
        code
            directory.open[Watch]():
              watch.stream.head
      alternative Polling
        code
            given Watcher = watchers.polling(0.5*Second)

            directory.open[Watch]():
              watch.stream.head
      ##
      ```

      `javaBaseWatcher` is a given, so it must be imported by name: `import watchers.*` does
      not import givens.
  entry .javaBaseWatcher   the operating system's notification service, `JavaBaseWatcher`, as a given
  entry .polling   makes a `PollingWatcher` which scans at the interval given
      `watchers.polling(interval)` takes any duration value and returns a `Watcher`. It is a
      method, not a given, so it is bound locally: `given Watcher = watchers.polling(0.05*Second)`.
